content.thinking:
All the uses of `BivvyConfig::default()` in non-test code are in list.rs and lint.rs, and they only handle `ConfigNotFound`, not `ConfigParseError`. So they're not the bug.

Let me look at `parse_config` more carefully. The function parses YAML content into BivvyConfig:

```rust
pub fn parse_config(content: &str, source_path: &Path) -> Result<BivvyConfig> {
    serde_yaml::from_str(content).map_err(|e| BivvyError::ConfigParseError {
        path: source_path.to_path_buf(),
        message: e.to_string(),
    })
}
```

With `#[serde(default)]` on BivvyConfig, deserializing an empty document or null gives you the default struct since all fields are optional. But if I have something like invalid keys in the YAML with `deny_unknown_fields` enabled, that would trigger a parse error instead of silently using defaults.

The issue is that `deny_unknown_fields` only applies to the top-level BivvyConfig struct. If StepConfig doesn't have the same protection, then invalid fields nested under steps would just be ignored without any validation. I need to check whether StepConfig has `deny_unknown_fields` defined.

